    "title": "How to Build a Content Strategy That Actually Drives Revenue",
    "slug": "1",
    "content": "Most content strategies look impressive in a slide deck and do nothing for the business. They optimize for traffic, shares, and follower counts — metrics that feel good in a dashboard but never show up in revenue. After auditing dozens of B2B content programs, the pattern is clear: the teams that tie content to pipeline win, and the rest burn budget.\n\nThe fix isn't more content. It's a strategy that connects every asset to a stage of the buyer journey and a measurable business outcome. Here's the framework we use to build content strategies that actually move revenue.\n\n## Start With Revenue, Not Topics\n\nBefore brainstorming a single topic, define what success means in dollars. How much pipeline does content need to influence this quarter? What's the average deal size, and how many opportunities must content generate to hit the target? Work backward from revenue to the number of qualified leads, then to the content needed to produce them.\n\nThis single shift — from 'what should we write about?' to 'what content moves pipeline?' — eliminates 80% of the wasted effort in most content programs.\n\n## Map Content to the Buyer Journey\n\nEvery piece of content should serve a stage: awareness, consideration, or decision. Top-of-funnel content attracts the right audience and introduces the problem. Mid-funnel content builds trust and differentiates your approach. Bottom-funnel content removes objections and accelerates the close.\n\nAudit your existing content against this map. You'll usually find a glut of awareness content and a famine of decision-stage assets — exactly the gap that lets deals stall.\n\n## Measure What Matters\n\nStop reporting pageviews as a success metric. Track content-sourced pipeline, content-influenced deals, and conversion rates from content to opportunity. Set up attribution that connects a blog visit to a demo request to a closed-won deal. When leadership sees content driving revenue, the content budget stops being the first thing cut.",
    "excerpt": "Most content strategies fail because they focus on vanity metrics. Here's a framework that ties every piece of content to pipeline generation.",
