    "title": "The Rise of Account-Based Content Marketing",
    "slug": "2",
    "content": "Account-based marketing promised personalization at scale, but for years it meant little more than slapping a target company's logo on a landing page. That's changing fast. The next wave of ABM is account-based content — building entire content experiences tailored to a single account's pain points, tech stack, and buying committee.\n\nIt sounds expensive, and done wrong it is. Done right, it compresses sales cycles and wins deals your outbound team never could. Here's how the leaders are pulling it off.\n\n## Beyond the Logo Swap\n\nReal account-based content starts with research, not content. Before writing a word, map the account's strategic priorities, recent news, executive speeches, and the specific metrics each stakeholder owns. The content then speaks directly to their world — citing their industry's benchmarks, their competitor's moves, and the exact problems their team is on the hook to solve.\n\nThe output might be a custom microsite, a benchmark report built from their own data, or a tailored ROI model. The format matters less than the relevance.\n\n## Build a Content Factory for One\n\nThe reason most teams don't do this is throughput. Personalized content for dozens of accounts feels impossible. The trick is a modular system: research templates that take hours not weeks, reusable data frameworks, and AI-assisted first drafts that a human editor refines for each account.\n\nTeams that industrialize this research-to-draft pipeline produce account-specific content at a cost that finally makes sense for anything but your top-tier targets.\n\n## Tie It to Pipeline\n\nAccount-based content only earns its budget when you measure account-level engagement against deal velocity. Track how target accounts interact with the content, how fast they move from stage to stage, and how the content changes meeting acceptance rates. When the data shows accounts converting faster, the program funds itself.",
