    "title": "Why Your Blog Needs a Video Component in 2026",
    "slug": "3",
    "content": "A blog post is no longer a wall of text. Readers skim, bounce, and rarely reach the second paragraph. The posts that hold attention in 2026 pair sharp writing with video — short, embedded clips that explain the idea the moment a reader's eyes want to wander.\n\nOur internal data shows articles with an embedded video keep readers on the page 88% longer and convert demo requests at roughly double the rate of text-only posts. The reason is simple: video does the heavy lifting of explanation while text does the heavy lifting of credibility.\n\n## Video Belongs in the Body, Not Just the Header\n\nMost teams drop a hero video at the top and call it done. The bigger win is contextual video — a 30-second clip that appears exactly where a concept gets complex. A reader who would have bounced at a dense paragraph instead watches a quick explanation and keeps reading.\n\nThese clips don't need a studio. A screen recording with a voiceover, lightly edited, outperforms polished video that takes six weeks to produce and ships two months late.\n\n## Let the Format Match the Idea\n\nNot every post needs video, and forcing it makes content worse. Use video for demos, walkthroughs, and anything where motion makes the point faster than words. Use text for frameworks, data, and ideas that need to be scanned and referenced later.\n\nThe discipline is choosing the format that gets the idea across fastest — and in 2026, that's increasingly a hybrid.\n\n## Measure Attention, Not Just Views\n\nA video play count is a vanity metric. What matters is whether the video kept the reader on the page long enough to reach your call to action. Pair your analytics so you can see time-on-page and conversion side by side with video engagement, and double down on the formats that actually move readers toward the goal.",
