    "title": "Content Repurposing: Turn One Piece Into Twenty",
    "slug": "4",
    "content": "The most expensive mistake in content marketing is treating every asset as a one-off. You spend weeks on a research report, publish it once, and then start the next project from a blank page. Meanwhile the report's insights could have fueled a month of social posts, a webinar, a podcast episode, and an email series — all from work you already did.\n\nRepurposing isn't recycling. It's extracting every drop of value from the research and writing you've already invested in. Here's the system I use to turn one cornerstone asset into twenty derivative pieces.\n\n## Start From a Cornerstone, Not a Feed\n\nA cornerstone is a substantial, original asset: a research report, a long-form guide, a recorded webinar. Everything else flows from it. When you start from a cornerstone, every derivative piece inherits its research and credibility, and you never face a blank page wondering what to post.\n\nPlan one cornerstone per month and map its derivatives before you even finish writing it.\n\n## The Extraction Map\n\nFrom one cornerstone, extract in three directions: atomic insights (each data point or framework becomes its own social post or thread), narrative pieces (the story arcs become a podcast episode and a newsletter), and visual assets (charts become carousels, frameworks become infographics).\n\nOne research report realistically yields a launch post, five social threads, two carousels, one podcast, two newsletter editions, and a video recap — twenty touchpoints from one effort.\n\n## Systematize, Don't Improvise\n\nThe reason repurposing fails is that teams treat it as an afterthought. Build it into the production process: when the cornerstone is approved, the derivative briefs are written the same week, and the channels are scheduled before the cornerstone publishes. Repurposing only works when it's a system, not a hope.",
