    "title": "SEO in the Age of AI: What Still Works",
    "slug": "6",
    "content": "AI overviews have absorbed the top of the search results page, and a lot of SEO playbooks broke overnight. The queries that used to deliver a tidy click are now answered in place, and traffic to informational pages has thinned. But search isn't dead — the rules just shifted from 'rank for the question' to 'be the source the AI trusts to answer it.'\n\nThe sites still growing organic traffic in 2026 share a few traits. Here's what actually works now.\n\n## Be the Original Source, Not the Summarizer\n\nAI overviews synthesize existing content; they don't generate original research. The pages that earn citations — and the clicks that survive — are the ones with data, quotes, and perspectives that don't exist anywhere else. If your page is a rephrasing of five other pages, the AI has no reason to credit you.\n\nOriginal research, proprietary data, and firsthand expert commentary are the new on-page SEO.\n\n## Win the Queries AI Can't Answer\n\nAI overviews are weakest on commercial intent, local results, and experiences that require trust or recency. Reviews, comparisons, case studies, and 'how we did it' content convert far better than generic explainers. Shift your effort toward the query types where a human voice and specificity still win.\n\n## Structure for Machine and Human\n\nClear headings, concise answers up front, and schema markup help both the AI summarize your page and the human skim it. The technical basics — fast pages, clean markup, descriptive titles — matter more now, not less, because they determine whether you're the page the AI chooses to cite.\n\n## Measure Citations, Not Just Clicks\n\nAdd 'appears in AI overview' as a tracked outcome alongside rankings and clicks. A page that earns a citation but no direct click is still building brand authority at the top of the funnel. The teams that adapt fastest are the ones measuring the new wins, not mourning the old metrics.",
    "excerpt": "Google's AI overviews are changing search. Here's what content marketers need to know to stay visible and drive organic traffic.",
