    "title": "Data-Driven Content: From Analytics to Action",
    "slug": "8",
    "content": "Most content teams drowning in dashboards still plan their editorial calendar on gut feel. They collect the data but never close the loop from 'what the data says' to 'what we publish next.' The result is a calendar full of topics that felt right and performed mediocre.\n\nThe fix is a simple weekly ritual that turns analytics into editorial decisions. Here's how to run it.\n\n## Find the Topics That Over-Perform\n\nPull your last quarter of content and rank it by conversion, not by traffic. You'll usually find a small set of topics that punch far above their weight — modest traffic but high pipeline. Those topics are your editorial priority. Double the cadence on them and stop funding the high-traffic, zero-conversion topics that inflate vanity metrics.\n\n## Read the Drop-off, Not Just the Click\n\nTraffic tells you the topic was interesting; time-on-page tells you whether you delivered. A page with high clicks and a 15-second dwell time is a promise you broke. Treat those pages as research: the topic earned the visit, so rewrite the page to actually satisfy the intent the headline set up.\n\n## Build a Feedback Loop Into the Calendar\n\nEach week, review the three best- and worst-performing pieces and write one editorial rule from each: 'comparison pages convert, schedule two more,' or 'listicles bounce, stop commissioning them.' Over a quarter, you build a living playbook that makes the calendar smarter even as people rotate off the team.\n\n## Measure the Output, Not the Effort\n\nA data-driven content team is measured by how quickly analytics change what gets published — not by how many reports it produces. If the dashboards aren't changing the calendar, they're decoration. The goal of data is a better plan, not a prettier deck.",
