    "title": "The Complete Guide to Thought Leadership Content",
    "slug": "9",
    "content": "'Thought leadership' has become a label for anything an executive publishes. Real thought leadership is rarer and more valuable: content that advances the conversation in an industry by taking a defensible, original position. Most of what's labeled thought leadership is actually thought followership — restating the consensus in louder type.\n\nThe content that earns the label follows a few disciplines. Here's the complete guide to building it.\n\n## Have a Point Worth Arguing With\n\nThought leadership requires a claim someone could disagree with. 'AI is changing marketing' is a truism. 'Most marketing teams should stop publishing and start curating' is a position. If your thesis can't be argued against, it can't lead thought. Start by stating the contrarian claim plainly, then earn it.\n\n## Back the Claim With Evidence\n\nAn opinion without evidence is a hot take. The leadership comes from the work: proprietary data, a framework tested with real clients, a pattern you've observed across dozens of deals. The evidence is what separates a thought leader from a loud one. Cite your sources and show the reasoning.\n\n## Earn the Right to the Topic\n\nAudiences grant authority to people who've done the work, not people who've read about it. The most credible thought leadership comes from practitioners describing what they've actually shipped — the campaigns that worked, the ones that didn't, and what changed because of them. Borrowed expertise reads as borrowed.\n\n## Take the Risk of Being Wrong\n\nThe thought leadership that spreads is the kind that commits. Hedged, committee-approved content designed to offend no one also moves no one. Publish the strong claim, attach your name to it, and be willing to update it when the evidence changes. The willingness to be wrong in public is exactly what audiences trust.",
